      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© The Author(s) 2014 Reprints and permissions: sagepub.co.uk/journalsPermissions.nav.",
        "AbstractText": "This case report describes an atypical and unique presentation of mesenteric arteries occlusive disease. The patient presented with typical symptoms of chronic mesenteric ischemia, as well as with an atypical new symptom; postprandial buttock and lower limbs pain. Pain followed the time curve of the postprandial abdominal discomfort, starting 30\u2009min after meals and gradually resolving within 2\u2009h. The patient had been tolerating the signs of chronic mesenteric ischemia quite well by adjusting the quantity of food per meal to relieve symptoms. Angiography showed that the celiac artery, the superior mesenteric artery, and distal aorta were occluded, leaving the inferior mesenteric artery as the only feeding vessel of all abdominal viscera and both the lower limbs. Since an English medical literature search returned only one marginally similar case, we consider this case of iliac arteries' \"steal syndrome\" from the inferior mesenteric artery unique."
      },
